WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.078
The last lesson in M4 is concept-dense because it asks students to combine two pressure sources into one clean total.

2
00:00:06.078 --> 00:00:12.156
The atmosphere is not empty nothingness above the liquid.

3
00:00:12.156 --> 00:00:18.235
It is a fluid blanket of air pressing on the open surface.

4
00:00:18.235 --> 00:00:24.313
That means a point inside an open liquid is not carrying only the pressure from the liquid above it.

5
00:00:24.313 --> 00:00:30.391
It is carrying atmospheric pressure plus the extra hydrostatic contribution from the liquid column.

6
00:00:30.391 --> 00:00:36.469
Once that story is secure, the final worked example stops feeling like a formula trick and starts feeling like a layered load picture.

7
00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:42.547
Keep this takeaway in view: Open-liquid pressure becomes easier when students think in layers: atmosphere first, liquid depth second.

8
00:00:42.547 --> 00:00:48.625
At the open surface means already p_atm.

9
00:00:48.625 --> 00:00:54.704
The atmosphere sets the starting pressure before depth is added.

10
00:00:54.704 --> 00:01:00.782
Deeper in the liquid means add rho g h.

11
00:01:00.782 --> 00:01:06.860
The liquid column contributes extra pressure below the surface.

12
00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:13.949
This diagram matters because it stops students from resetting the pressure to zero at the liquid surface.

13
00:01:13.949 --> 00:01:21.038
In an open tank, the air above the liquid is pressing down already.

14
00:01:21.038 --> 00:01:28.127
So the surface pressure is atmospheric pressure.

15
00:01:28.127 --> 00:01:35.216
Move deeper and the liquid adds more pressure on top of that starting value.

16
00:01:35.216 --> 00:01:42.304
The result is total pressure equal to atmospheric pressure plus rho g h.

17
00:01:42.304 --> 00:01:49.393
This picture is especially useful in worked examples where students might remember rho g h but forget what the open surface is already carrying before the depth term begins.

18
00:01:49.393 --> 00:01:56.482
Keep this takeaway in view: The diagram is strongest when students can say what is present at the surface before talking about what depth adds.

19
00:01:56.482 --> 00:02:03.571
Surface condition: At the open surface, the pressure is atmospheric pressure.

20
00:02:03.571 --> 00:02:10.660
Depth condition: Going deeper adds the liquid contribution rho g h.

21
00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:15.935
A helpful analogy is to imagine a point in the liquid lying under two blankets.

22
00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:21.210
The first blanket is the atmosphere, which covers the whole open surface from above.

23
00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:26.485
The second blanket is the liquid column directly above the point.

24
00:02:26.485 --> 00:02:31.760
At the surface you already feel the air blanket.

25
00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:37.035
As you move deeper, the water blanket gets thicker and heavier, so the total load increases.

26
00:02:37.035 --> 00:02:42.310
This analogy is useful because it prevents students from treating atmospheric pressure as optional decoration.

27
00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:47.585
In an open liquid, it is part of the physical load from the start.

28
00:02:47.585 --> 00:02:52.860
Keep this takeaway in view: The double-blanket picture helps students keep the atmospheric term in the story instead of dropping it too early.

29
00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:58.135
Blanket one means air load.

30
00:02:58.135 --> 00:03:03.410
Atmospheric pressure is present before depth is considered.

31
00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:08.685
Blanket two means liquid load.

32
00:03:08.685 --> 00:03:13.960
The liquid column adds a depth-dependent contribution.

33
00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:20.230
The key equation is p total equals p atmospheric plus rho g h.

34
00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:26.500
The first term is the pressure applied by the air at the open surface.

35
00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:32.770
The second term is the extra pressure caused by the liquid column above the point.

36
00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:39.040
If the atmosphere stays the same and the depth increases, only the rho g h part changes.

37
00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:45.310
If the depth stays the same but atmospheric conditions change, the baseline shifts everywhere in the liquid.

38
00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:51.580
This richer reading matters because some questions ask for total pressure while others ask only for the liquid contribution.

39
00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:57.850
Keep this takeaway in view: The formula becomes memorable when students hear it as atmospheric baseline plus depth-dependent addition.

40
00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:04.120
At h = 0: The total pressure is p_atm at the open surface.

41
00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:10.390
As h increases: The liquid term grows while the atmospheric baseline may stay constant.

42
00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:16.660
p_total = p_atm + rho g h.

43
00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:22.930
p_atm is the starting surface pressure.

44
00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:29.200
Read carefully whether the question wants total pressure or just the pressure due to the liquid.

45
00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:34.642
Use a simple routine.

46
00:04:34.642 --> 00:04:40.083
First, ask whether the liquid is open to the atmosphere.

47
00:04:40.083 --> 00:04:45.525
If it is, begin the pressure story at p atmospheric.

48
00:04:45.525 --> 00:04:50.967
Second, measure the depth below the free surface, not below the floor or the container base.

49
00:04:50.967 --> 00:04:56.408
Third, add rho g h for the liquid contribution.

50
00:04:56.408 --> 00:05:01.850
If two points are compared in the same open liquid, notice that the atmospheric part may be common to both, which can help comparisons even though it is still physically present.

51
00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:07.292
This routine gives students a dependable map for the final worked example.

52
00:05:07.292 --> 00:05:12.733
Keep this takeaway in view: The open-liquid routine helps students keep both the starting baseline and the added depth term visible.

53
00:05:12.733 --> 00:05:18.175
Step 1 means is it open?.

54
00:05:18.175 --> 00:05:23.617
An open surface means atmospheric pressure is the starting point.

55
00:05:23.617 --> 00:05:29.058
Step 2 means measure h correctly.

56
00:05:29.058 --> 00:05:34.500
Depth is measured below the liquid surface, not from the tank bottom.

57
00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:39.157
Try four checks.

58
00:05:39.157 --> 00:05:43.815
At the open surface, is the pressure zero?

59
00:05:43.815 --> 00:05:48.472
No, it is atmospheric pressure.

60
00:05:48.472 --> 00:05:53.129
Deeper in the same liquid, does the total pressure increase?

61
00:05:53.129 --> 00:05:57.787
Yes, because rho g h increases.

62
00:05:57.787 --> 00:06:02.444
If the depth is unchanged but atmospheric pressure changed, would the total pressure change too?

63
00:06:02.444 --> 00:06:07.101
Yes, because the baseline has shifted.

64
00:06:07.101 --> 00:06:11.759
And if a question asks only for the liquid contribution, should p atmospheric still be written in the final numerical answer?

65
00:06:11.759 --> 00:06:16.416
No, not unless total pressure is required.

66
00:06:16.416 --> 00:06:21.073
Those distinctions matter a great deal in final pressure questions.

67
00:06:21.073 --> 00:06:25.731
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once students can keep the atmospheric baseline and the liquid addition distinct.

68
00:06:25.731 --> 00:06:30.388
Open surface means p = p_atm.

69
00:06:30.388 --> 00:06:35.045
The surface is not a zero-pressure location.

70
00:06:35.045 --> 00:06:39.703
Go deeper means total rises.

71
00:06:39.703 --> 00:06:44.360
The liquid contribution adds to the same atmospheric baseline.

72
00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:51.416
You are ready for the final worked example now.

73
00:06:51.416 --> 00:06:58.472
Begin by deciding whether the liquid is open to the atmosphere.

74
00:06:58.472 --> 00:07:05.528
If it is, state that the pressure at the surface is p atmospheric.

75
00:07:05.528 --> 00:07:12.583
Then measure the depth below that surface and add rho g h for the liquid contribution.

76
00:07:12.583 --> 00:07:19.639
Finally, read the question again to check whether it wants total pressure, the pressure due to the liquid alone, or a comparison between points.

77
00:07:19.639 --> 00:07:26.695
The strongest solutions in this lesson are not just accurate; they are explicit about which pressure is being reported and why.

78
00:07:26.695 --> 00:07:33.751
Keep this takeaway in view: The final worked example is ready: open-liquid pressure can now be read as atmospheric baseline plus hydrostatic addition.

79
00:07:33.751 --> 00:07:40.807
Surface move: Never begin an open-liquid pressure story as though the surface pressure were zero.

80
00:07:40.807 --> 00:07:47.862
Depth move: Use depth below the free surface, because that is what controls the liquid addition.

81
00:07:47.862 --> 00:07:54.918
start with p_atm at the open surface.

82
00:07:54.918 --> 00:08:01.974
measure h below the surface and add rho g h.

83
00:08:01.974 --> 00:08:09.030
Many mistakes disappear when the solution states the surface condition before substituting numbers.
